{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 8, :comments ["<b>Elohim</b> — All the commentators explained that at the time of the giving of the Torah the heavens and earth quaked, and so too 'Eloah shall come from Teman' and so 'YHWH came from Sinai' and so 'YHWH, when You went forth from Seir,' and so 'Elohim, when You went forth before Your people.' But this verse will refute them, for it is impossible to say of the giving of the Torah 'when You strode through the wilderness, selah' — for ישימון is from the root of והבמות תשמנה (Isaiah 32:13-14), and when Israel was at Sinai they were in settled land, not in the waste, the howling wilderness, which is the great and terrible desert, in a land through which no man had passed, and this was after their departure from Mount Sinai. Furthermore, it is not written in the Torah that the earth quaked, nor the heavens, on the day of the giving of the Torah — only Mount Sinai alone trembled. And this is the meaning of the verse written in the Torah, 'YHWH came from Sinai': from Sinai He began to come into the camp of Israel, and His might was not seen until after they encircled Mount Seir, as written, and Paran was near them, for so it is written, 'and the Horite in their mountain, Seir, as far as El-paran.' And this is 'when You strode through the field of Edom,' and so 'Eloah shall come from Teman,' which is like 'Teman says.'" "<b>And the meaning of 'when You went forth before Your people'</b> — the going of the Ark ahead, as when they departed from Mount Sinai." "<b>And the meaning of 'the earth quaked'</b> — like 'and the mountains trembled' (Habakkuk 3:10), that all the nations were afraid. Do you not see in David's song he said, 'the earth shook and trembled' and 'the foundations' (Psalm 18:8)? And the meaning: it was almost as though the entire world was overturned, and because the earth was standing, he said the earth quaked just as Sinai quaked on the day of the giving of the Torah because of HaShem. And Rabbi Moses said: 'for this Sinai' is its meaning — behold this refers to the pillar of glory upon it." "<b>And the meaning of 'the Elohim of Israel'</b> — for on account of Israel did Elohim descend upon Mount Sinai."], :book :psalms, :voice :ibn-ezra, :lang :en, :chapter 68, :notes {0 "Ibn Ezra quotes or paraphrases several verses in rapid succession; the references (Judges 5:4, Deut. 33:2, Habakkuk 3:3, etc.) are left implicit in the source and preserved here without insertion.", 2 "Rabbi Moses here apparently identifies the referent of זה סיני in Judges 5:5 with the pillar of cloud/glory; the reading is preserved hyper-literally."}, :source-witnesses [["Ibn Ezra on Psalms -- Daat" "http://www.daat.ac.il/he-il/tanach/parshanut_hamikra/full-text/eben-ezra/tehilim"] ["Wikisource Mikraot Gedolot" "http://en.wikisource.org/wiki/Mikraot_Gedolot/"] ["Tanakh, Venice, 1617-1619" "https://dlib.rsl.ru/viewer/01007514045#?page=4"]], :timestamp "2026-07-09T19:33:12.110207241Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}